.he "ln (1)                       8/16/26                       ln (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
ln
- make links
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
ln [ -f ] sourcename [ targetname ]
.bd
ln [ -f ] sourcename1 sourcename2 ... targetdirectory
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
A link is a directory entry that refers to a file; the same file, with
its size, protection and the rest, may have several links to it.  A
hard link is indistinguishable from the original entry, and a change
to the file shows through either name.  Hard links may not span file
systems and may not refer to directories.
.sp
Given one or two arguments, ln makes a link to sourcename.  If
targetname is given, the link has that name; if targetname is a
directory, the link is put in it under the last part of sourcename.
With no targetname, the link is made in the current directory.
.sp
Given more than two arguments, ln makes links in targetdirectory to
all the named source files, each under its own name.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-f	Make the link even when the source is a directory.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The -s option is gone with the symbolic links it would have made:
micronix has no symbolic links, so ln says so and exits rather than
silently making a hard link a script did not want.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
rm (1), cp (1), mv (1)
